  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A deepening trough will result in a \nstrengthening coastal low providing a shot of rain on Thursday \nwith the potential for a transition to snow in the northeastern \nzones Thursday night.\n\nThe main game in town across central Pennsylvania over the next\nweek will be the potential for the first accumulating snowfall \nmainly for the higher elevations of central Pennsylvania. As a \nlow pressure system approaches the region from the Ohio Valley, \npotential for scattered showers increase this evening into \ntonight. Signals continue for the developing surface low \npressure to be sub-1000mb as it moves just south of the region \non Thursday. All guidance points towards increasing moisture \nwith southeasterly flow from the Chesapeake Bay into the \nSusquehanna Valley, which will promote more widespread shower \nactivity. Current thinking is that there will continue to be \nsome orographic enhancement with recent REFS ensemble guidance \noutlining two six-hour blocks (between 12Z Thursday and 00Z \nFriday) with low-end probabilities of exceeding one inch of QPF \nacross the Middle Susquehanna Valley where more forcing and \norographic lift will be in place compared to the Lower \nSusquehanna Valley. Not entirely sold on there being over an \ninch in any six-hour block; however, this signal does outline \nthe heavy rain potential. No flooding concerns will exist with \nwell-below average precipitation across the entire area over the\nlast two months. \n\nModel guidance has also stayed consistent with respect to cold\nair wrapping around the back side of this system. The mentions\nof frontogenetic forcing, which will bring about lingering\nshowers across the northeastern zones mentioned in the previous\nforecast cycle remains fairly consistent. Current forecast has a\nslight uptick in snowfall across our northeastern zones\n(Potter/Tioga/Sullivan), where recent REFS total snowfall\nprobabilities have a greater than 60% chance for receiving\naccumulations over one inch. Some isolated instances of\naccumulations reaching into the 2-3\" range across the higher\nelevations of Sullivan County have been outlined in the previous\ntwo forecast cycles, but there's still questions on how long\nprecipitation lingers across the region. \n\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Northwesterly flow brings much cooler \ntemperatures this weekend, with lake-enhanced snow showers \ncontinuing Friday into Saturday night.\n\nCooler temperatures and breezy northwesterly winds will take \nhold of the region as the area of low pressure shifts east \ntowards the New England Coast. Wind gusts in the 25-35 mph range\nare likely from Thursday night through the weekend. By sunrise \non Friday, the stratiform precip will be pulling away and north-\nsouth oriented lake effect streamer bands will likely be in its\nwake. Typically streamer bands will trend towards a more \ncellular mode with daytime heating. At this time, snowfall \nappears to be the dominant precipitation type across the \nnorthern/western halves of Pennsylvania with rain prevailing \nelsewhere. That being said cannot completely rule out a \nrain/snow mix further south/east if precipitation rates trend \nhigher. The lake effect and upslope snow on the back side of \nthis system is expected to produce a coating to one inch of snow\nacross the Laurels and the northwest mountains with localized \namounts pushing closer to two inches in the highest elevations.\n\nChances for precipitation begin to decrease Saturday night into \nSunday as a subsidence inversion builds in with approaching high\npressure. A slight warming trend emerges into the beginning of \nnext week, with temperatures trending closer to climatological \naverages by Tuesday.",
